<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  INT-032 TEST FIXTURE. Not a second operational template.

  This file is `packages/protocols/openehr/previsit_intake.v1.opt` — the
  committed, sha-pinned template — plus ONE structural element per content
  child: `<occurrences>`. Nothing else differs. No `atNNNN` code, no archetype,
  no rm_type, no clinical content is added, and the nine archetype roots and
  their RM types are identical (asserted by
  tests/integration/test_ehrbase_roundtrip.py, which parses BOTH files with the
  committed `protocols.bindings.openehr.load_opt` walker and compares).

  WHY IT EXISTS. EHRbase 2.35.1 refuses the committed file with HTTP 500 and
  `java.lang.NullPointerException: Cannot invoke
  "org.openehr.schemas.v1.IntervalOfInteger.getLowerUnbounded()" because
  "occurrences" is null` — AOM 1.4 `C_OBJECT.occurrences` is optional in
  Template.xsd but EHRbase/Archie dereferences it without a default. The
  committed template declares `occurrences` on the COMPOSITION root and on no
  child. That is a gap in packages/protocols, reported by INT-032 and NOT
  fixed here: this issue may consume that package, not edit it.

  The occurrences chosen are the least-constraining interval AOM can express,
  `0..*` (lower 0, included; upper unbounded). It adds no constraint the RM
  does not already impose, so it asserts nothing about how many reasons for
  encounter or symptoms an intake may carry — a decision nobody has taken.

  The production client (`intake_platform.ehrbase`) never reads this file. It
  uploads `OPT_PATH` verbatim and raises `EhrbaseTemplateRejectedError` when
  the CDR refuses it; `test_the_committed_template_is_rejected_today` pins that
  refusal. When packages/protocols adopts the same element, that test flips and
  this fixture is deleted.
-->
<template xmlns="http://schemas.openehr.org/v1" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance">
